          "section_id": "c_sections.0",
          "heading": "why_in_hospital",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "Your child came in with two weeks of drinking a lot and passing a lot of urine, along with losing 3 kg in weight. This was followed by two days of vomiting, tummy pain, and drowsiness.\n\nThe team found that your child had diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). This means the blood had become too acidic because of a lack of insulin. The team also found that your child has type 1 diabetes mellitus, which is a new diagnosis."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "On arrival, your child's blood sugar was very high and the blood was acidic. The team started a DKA treatment plan. This included:\n\n- Fluids given carefully through a drip\n- Insulin given through a drip\n- Potassium replacement through the drip\n- Checks every hour\n\nThe intensive care team (PICU) was informed. Your child was cared for in the high-dependency area of the ward.\n\nBy Day 2, the blood was less acidic and the ketones had come down. Your child was moved on to insulin given by injection under the skin, using a basal-bolus regimen.\n\nThe diabetes nurse and dietician started working with your family. Education has begun, covering carbohydrate counting, how to manage low blood sugar (hypos), and what to do when your child is unwell (sick-day rules)."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{MEDICATIONS}}"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.3",
          "heading": "next_steps",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.7",
            "sections.8"
          ],
          "text": "- The diabetes team will follow up in 1 week.\n- Phone support is available in the meantime.\n- A dietician follow-up has been arranged.\n- Your child's GP has been made aware. No urgent action is needed from the GP at this time."
        },
